{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"reciprocal conversion factors reciprocal conversion factors","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","fileName":"Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Reciprocal Conversion Factors","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 18:36\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Unit Algebra and Measurement Precision]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Unit Algebra and Measurement Precision","Temperature Readings versus Intervals","dimensionalanalysisforunitconversionusingmatlab.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"reciprocal conversion factors reciprocal conversion factors","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","fileName":"Reciprocal Conversion Factors.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Reciprocal Conversion Factors","heading":"Reciprocal Conversion Factors","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A factor for one direction of a compatible conversion can be inverted for the reverse direction. Pryor's fuel-efficiency example uses a kilometers-per-mile ratio in the numerator but the reciprocal of liters-per-gallon in the denominator, so gallons cancel and liters remain below kilometers. Bidirectional conversion still requires choosing the correct orientation for each unit appearing in a compound expression. A reciprocal does not by itself handle scales with an offset, such as absolute Fahrenheit and Celsius readings. See [[Temperature Readings versus Intervals]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Unit Algebra and